.TH "UMAMI" "1" "2026" "umami 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
umami \- filter repetitive log events by structure
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]umami\f[R] \f[I]SUBCOMMAND\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]]
[\f[I]ARGS\f[R]\&...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
umami groups events by fixed words and parameter positions.
It reports new structures, unusual minute counts, stable\-parameter
changes, and silence.
A previously learned error can recur without an alert; use explicit
rules for known failures.
.PP
The independent tokenizer recognizes timestamps, numbers, IP addresses,
quoted contents, JSON keys, and key=value pairs.
Variable\-looking text includes digits, paths, URLs, email addresses,
long hexadecimal identifiers, and dotted host:port values.
Exact signatures retain parameter positions.
The Legacy tokenizer and compatibility claim have been removed.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.SS tokenize
\f[B]umami tokenize\f[R] [\f[B]\(enpretty\f[R] \f[I]STYLE\f[R]]
[\f[B]\(encolor\f[R]] [\f[B]\(entest\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
[\f[B]\(enfix\f[R]] [\f[B]\(enverbose\f[R]]] [\f[I]LINE\f[R]\&...]
.PP
Inspect arguments or standard input.
Styles are \f[B]test\f[R], \f[B]colors\f[R], \f[B]params\f[R],
\f[B]delimiters\f[R], and \f[B]static\f[R].
The default test style renders typed tokens; static renders fixed text
with **<*>** for parameters.
Use \(encolor with colors for ANSI output.
.PP
\(entest reads input/expected TSV pairs.
\(enverbose also prints passing cases.
\(enfix rewrites expectations in the supplied file.
Never use it on original reference captures; review changes on a copy of
an independent regression fixture instead.
.IP
.EX
umami tokenize \-\-pretty static \(aqrequest id=42 took 3 ms\(aq
umami tokenize \-\-test umami/corpus/structure.tsv
.EE
.SS pipeline
\f[B]umami pipeline\f[R] [\f[I]DETECT OPTIONS\f[R]]
[\f[B]\(enshow\-all\f[R]]
.PP
Print each anomaly kind followed by its original event.
With \(enshow\-all, print normal events as \f[B]ok\f[R].
Synthetic stop and away alerts print the system and structure instead of
a source line.
A multiline event retains embedded newlines.
.SS sim
\f[B]umami sim\f[R] [\f[I]DETECT OPTIONS\f[R]]
.PP
Print event count, anomaly count, suppression percentage, profile count,
and counts by kind.
Synthetic sweep alerts do not count as input events.
In multiline mode, events are assembled messages rather than physical
lines.
.SS ingest
\f[B]umami ingest\f[R] [\f[I]DETECT OPTIONS\f[R]]
.PP
Print one JSON object per anomaly, with \f[B]system\f[R],
\f[B]kind\f[R], \f[B]score\f[R], \f[B]hash\f[R], \f[B]signature\f[R],
\f[B]line\f[R], and \f[B]changed_params\f[R].
The hash is null for system\-away events; stop and away events have
empty source lines.
Changed parameters use zero\-based dynamic slot ordinals, including
positions occupied by excluded timestamps.
Scores are fixed values by kind, not probabilities.
.SS load
\f[B]umami load\f[R] [\f[B]\(encount\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]]
[\f[B]\(enstdin\f[R]]
.PP
Run a throughput probe.
Default input contains one million synthetic events from four templates;
\(enstdin reads actual input.
This measures filtering speed, not detection quality.
.SH DETECT OPTIONS
The following apply to pipeline, sim, and ingest.
.TP
\f[B]\(ensystem\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
Attribute untagged input to this system (default: stdin).
.TP
\f[B]\(entagged\f[R]
Read system, tab, and message per physical line.
A line without a tab uses \(ensystem.
Cannot be combined with \(enmultiline.
.TP
\f[B]\(enparameters\f[R]
Report new values in stable parameter slots.
Requires twenty preceding observations and at most one distinct value
per ten observations.
Timestamp tokens are excluded; slots exceeding 100 distinct values
become permanently free\-form.
Without this option, no parameter\-value tracking occurs.
.TP
\f[B]\(enmultiline\f[R]
Join indented continuations to the preceding event.
Flush at EOF or after one second without input.
Events are limited to 1 MiB; exceeding the bound fails instead of
silently dropping content.
Late continuations can form separate events.
Only the first physical line supplies the timestamp.
.TP
\f[B]\(entime\-format\f[R] \f[I]FORMAT\f[R]
Parse a leading strftime\-style timestamp with an explicit year, date,
and time.
Numeric offsets are supported; zone\-less input uses UTC.
The parsed prefix becomes a timestamp token.
Invalid formats or mismatching input fail rather than falling back
silently to automatic recognition.
.TP
\f[B]\(enstate\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Resume learned profiles and save accepted learning on completion,
interruption, and periodic checkpoints.
A lifetime lock excludes another writer.
Atomic replacement uses a unique temporary file in the same directory.
Incompatible settings, malformed state, and old format versions fail
without replacing existing state.
Keep the file in a trusted directory.
Version 0.1 state requires relearning under a new path.
.SH TIME AND ALERTS
Automatic recognition supports ISO, Apache CLF, and syslog headers.
Yearless syslog input assumes substantially chronological order, with a
separate initial reference\-date rule and continuing New Year inference.
Arbitrary reordering without years remains ambiguous.
Timestamped replay does not advance just because real time passes.
Untimed input uses wall time until event\-time history supplies an
anchor.
.PP
Timestamps more than one day ahead of real time fail before changing
learned state.
This allowance does not make zone\-less timestamps timezone\-aware.
.PP
Frequency detection waits for thirty completed minute buckets.
Its threshold compares current count to the historical mean with a
variance floor.
A frequency_spike reports the first crossing, not the eventual size of a
flood.
.PP
Event\-stop detection requires twenty regular intervals, a standard
deviation no greater than one fifth of the mean, and silence beyond
three mean intervals.
Return gaps train the baseline.
Stop and spike alerts have independent rolling 24\-hour cooldowns.
Continuous silence is one episode.
System\-away defaults to ten minutes of absence.
.SH SHUTDOWN AND INPUT
Invalid UTF\-8 is replaced.
SIGINT and SIGTERM request a checkpoint of accepted learning.
A blocked output pipe must not prevent saving; shutdown may discard
queued output that cannot drain.
SIGKILL and power loss cannot trigger a final save, so they can lose
learning since the last checkpoint.
Neither a checkpoint nor input buffering guarantees downstream delivery
of every emitted alert.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
umami pipeline \-\-system web < syslog
journalctl \-f | umami pipeline \-\-state \(ti/.cache/umami.json
umami ingest \-\-parameters < syslog
umami ingest \-\-multiline < stacktraces.log
umami sim \-\-time\-format \(aq%Y/%m/%d %H:%M:%S\(aq < custom.log
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[B]umami/corpus/structure.tsv\f[R]
Independent tokenizer regression expectations.
.TP
\f[B]umami/corpus/reference/\f[R]
Original unomalycli reference captures, retained for provenance rather
than rewritten.
